# Per-node / per-deployment overrides for the docker-compose stack.
#
# Copy to deploy/.env (untracked) and fill in. Everything here is (a) used by
# compose for ${VAR} interpolation in docker-compose.yml, and (b) injected into
# every FastPKI container's environment. The config loader (Config::from_env)
# applies any key that is NOT set in the universal, git-tracked bootstrap.compose.conf,
# so per-node values live here and the committed config stays identical on every
# node. A single-node deployment can leave the per-node section unset.

# --- REQUIRED SECRETS — no defaults, both must be filled in ------------------
# ⚠️ FILL BOTH IN. They are blank on purpose, and leaving them blank is not safe: compose
# falls back to the well-known PIN `1234` for the SoftHSM token holding every CA private
# key, and to the password `fastpki` for Postgres. The only sign is a startup warning.
#
# FASTPKI_PIN is now fail-closed in certgen.sh. POSTGRES_PASSWORD still has a compose
# default (`${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:-fastpki}` in docker-compose.yml), so listing it here is
# what makes it a decision rather than an inherited value.
#
# deploy/install.sh generates both. Fill them in by hand only if you are not using it.
FASTPKI_PIN=
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# --- compose interpolation + per-deployment identity ------------------------
# Image: build node builds locally (fastpki:local); other nodes pull a tag.
FASTPKI_IMAGE=fastpki:local
# Postgres publish address. Loopback is safe for a single server. Set this server's own
# reachable IP on a mesh node (so peer data centers can subscribe) and on both servers of
# an HA pair (so the standby can connect; docs/deployment.md §6a).
PG_BIND=127.0.0.1
# Public FQDN of this deployment (drives BASE_URL / AIA / CRLDP + the web TLS CN, and the
# SANs certgen puts in the pre-CA transport certificate).
#
# ⚠️ Set it. Left unset, certgen issues the pre-CA transport certificate for `localhost`,
# and nothing complains — a certificate for the wrong name still verifies against itself.
#
# ⚠️ And make it a name RELYING PARTIES resolve, never this node's short hostname. It is
# baked into every issued certificate as the CRLDP and AIA URL, so a short name resolves
# on the node and inside the compose network — every check run there passes — and fails
# only on a client elsewhere, as
#     error 3 at 0 depth lookup: unable to get certificate CRL
# which reads as a CRL fault rather than a hostname setting. Certificates already issued
# keep the URL they were given.
PKI_DNS=pki.example.org

# --- publishing this deployment's token over mTLS ---
# p11-kit speaks neither TLS nor TCP — its client shim parses `unix:path=` and
# `vsock:cid=;port=` and links no bind/listen — so a token is reachable only by processes
# that can open one file on one host.
#
# This publishes THIS deployment's token so a peer can replicate a CA private key out of it
# into its own token. Every node keeps its own token either way; this is not a way to sign
# through somebody else's, which would stop all signing the moment that host was lost.
#
# P11_TLS=on makes certgen mint the pinned, self-signed server certificate — its private key
# as a `p11-server` object in the token, never a file — and the `p11tls` compose profile runs
# stunnel beside the token. Both are required, and both are off by default.
#
#   P11_TLS=on
#   COMPOSE_PROFILES=...,p11tls
#   docker compose run --rm certgen      # mints the material; a valid pair is a no-op
#
# ⚠️ WHAT CROSSES THE LINK. C_Login carries the PIN protecting every CA private key in the
# token. On one host that PIN is a 0400 file that never leaves the machine; over a tunnel it
# is on the wire, and client-certificate verification is the only thing between the network
# and every CA. Both ends verify the peer AND the chain, and the server trusts only the
# certificates in /var/pki/tls/p11/clients. There is deliberately no way to turn that off.
# P11_TLS=on
# P11_TLS_PORT=12345

# --- Active Directory: which DNS resolves the domain ---------------
# A directory is configured by NAME (`ldap://dc.corp.example`), and every AD-facing
# feature resolves that name first: LDAP login, importing users and groups, importing MS
# certificate templates, and Kerberos/SPNEGO.
#
# Docker's embedded resolver answers for compose service names and forwards everything else
# to the HOST's resolver — which, on a machine that is not domain-joined, has never heard of
# the AD zone. The failure does not look like DNS: the log says
#
#     ldap_list_groups: bind failed @ ldap://dc.corp.example: Can't contact LDAP server
#
# so it reads as a firewall or credentials problem. Confirm it with
# `docker compose exec web nslookup dc.corp.example` — NXDOMAIN from 127.0.0.11 is this.
#
# Point these at the domain controller's DNS. Unset, the keys are omitted entirely and
# resolution behaves exactly as before — which is the right answer for most production
# deployments, where the host already resolves the AD zone and containers inherit it.
#
# ⚠️ ONE ADDRESS. Compose hands this value to Docker as a single DNS server, and a
# comma-separated list stops every service from being created
# ("invalid DNS address: ParseAddr(\"10.10.0.10,10.10.0.11\")" — measured). Only the
# Kubernetes path (deploy/k8s/env.sh) splits a list. For two or more independent forests,
# point this at ONE resolver that does conditional forwarding per zone.
#
# ⚠️ Keep using the NAME in the directory's URI, not an IP: an `ldaps://` certificate is
# issued to the DC's name, and Kerberos needs SRV records only the AD DNS serves.
# DIRECTORY_DNS=192.0.2.53
# DIRECTORY_DNS_SEARCH=corp.example

# --- multi-data-center active-active: THIS node's serial slice -----
# Each node must mint certificate serials inside its own, non-overlapping slice
# of the 20-byte (40-hex) space, or the DB range guard (fastpki_dc_range_guard)
# rejects them. Split the space into N equal ranges, one per DC. Leave all three
# unset for a single node (full-range random serials).
# Set THIS node's id; the serial prefix it mints under comes from its own row in the
# `datacenters` table, which `fastpki-mesh --map` writes from the topology file. There
# is deliberately no prefix key here — one source of truth.
# ⚠️ An id with no matching row means this node REFUSES to issue. Apply --map first.
# DATACENTER_ID=1

# --- CMP client-certificate trust anchor -------------------------
# PEM bundle of the CA(s) whose client certs may sign CMP requests. Lives in the
# /var/pki volume. Unset = CMP accepts only PBM-secret-protected requests.
# CMP_CLIENT_CA_ID=<CA id>   # client-auth anchor: a registered CA (a `certs` row with is_ca), not a file

# --- Single sign-on: OIDC and SAML ------------------------------------------
# NOT configured here, and there is no key to uncomment. An issuer, a client secret,
# a redirect URI and a pinned IdP certificate all describe ONE identity provider, and
# this file can hold exactly one value per key — which is why a deployment could never
# name a second provider. They are rows in the database instead.
#
# Add them on the console's Directories page under "Federated sign-in", or through
# /api/auth-providers with kind=saml or kind=oidc. Both replicate to every node like the
# rest of the configuration, so this is set once for the deployment rather than per node.
#
# The OIDC client secret is a credential and lives in the database, write-only in the
# console — it is never returned by the API and never belongs in a file.

# --- which protocols the console reports as INSTALLED ------------------------
# Read by fastpki-web only, to fill the "installed" column on the Endpoints page —
# whether a container was ever built for that protocol. This is NOT what starts a
# service: COMPOSE_PROFILES (written by install.sh from the protocols you pick) is.
# So the two answer different questions, and the page keeps them apart: installed,
# enabled, started, and which certificate the listener serves are four distinct
# states. <PROTO>_ENABLED, in the config table, is the runtime switch.
# EST_INSTALLED=1
# ACME_INSTALLED=1
# CMP_INSTALLED=1
# MS_INSTALLED=1
# SCEP_INSTALLED=1
# STORE_INSTALLED=1

# --- SoftHSM token initialisation -------------------------------------------
# The token's security-officer PIN, used ONCE when the softhsm container creates
# the token and never by a FastPKI binary. Separate from FASTPKI_PIN, which is the
# user PIN every service signs with.
#
# ⚠️ It has a compose fallback of `0000`, so leaving this unset creates the token
# holding every CA private key under a well-known SO PIN. The SO PIN can reset the
# user PIN, so it is worth setting for the same reason FASTPKI_PIN is.
# HSM_SO_PIN=

# --- joining this node to another as its standby ----------------------------
# Read by the `postgres` service itself, not only by the `ha` profile: with STANDBY_OF
# set and an empty data directory, this node seeds from that host with pg_basebackup and
# then streams as a standby instead of initialising a fresh database. Once seeded it
# keeps following that host across restarts, so STANDBY_OF stays set.
#
# STANDBY_CA is the primary's trust anchor, and a standby on a SECOND HOST needs it:
# seeding uses sslmode=verify-full, and this node's own /var/pki/tls/pg/ca.crt is a
# self-signed certificate the primary was not issued by. Default
# /var/pki/tls/pg/primary-ca.crt, which `deploy/ha-join.sh` installs — run that script
# rather than setting these by hand; it checks the anchor really verifies the primary,
# and that the primary's certificate covers the address, before anything starts.
#
# ⚠️ Destructive on a data directory holding a former PRIMARY's database: that is a
# diverged timeline, so the node refuses to start rather than silently becoming a second
# read-write primary. Remove the data volume to re-seed, or unset STANDBY_OF to start on
# the data this node already has. See docs/high-availability.md 3.
# STANDBY_OF=<address of the node to seed from, as this node reaches it>
# STANDBY_CA=/var/pki/tls/pg/primary-ca.crt

# --- which protocol services start ---
# EVERY protocol service sits behind a compose profile, so a bare `docker compose up -d`
# starts postgres, token and the console ONLY — no enrolment, and no error saying so. Name
# the set you deploy here. install.sh writes this line from the protocols you picked.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=ocsp,est,acme,cmp,ms,store,scep
